s `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does, unless the PR's own patch to that path is unchanged: the stable patch id of the diff from the merge base is identical before and after the update, the head reaches the reviewed commit along first parents through two-parent merges only, and no git step failed. Every merge in that chain must also have exactly the tree of git's clean mechanical merge of its two parents (`git merge-tree --write-tree`, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, no renormalize), so a conflict resolution, a whitespace edit or a hand-crafted merge breaks the cover even when the patch id is unchanged; a later PR commit breaks it too, and any git error, including a git without `merge-tree --write-tree`, is not covered. The merger logs the paths covered this way as `merge.cover_patch_equal`; the reviewer's mark-ready and reviewfix landing keep the plain rule). A review made stale by code that changed since it is re-requested once per head: the merger removes `ard:review:<role>:done` (the reviewer then claims the role again at the current head), records a `review_rerequest` event for that PR and head, and logs `merge.review_rerequested`. A head that moved only by an update-branch merge from `main` is still covered and triggers nothing. Under dry-run it logs `merge.would_rerequest` and writes only to the le