{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017kBDaTN3K1wJseA1qqfgP1","type":"tool_result","content":" internal/servecmd/serve.go       |   3 +-\n internal/worker/conflict.go      | 175 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/worker/conflict_test.go |  38 +++++++++\n internal/worker/loop.go          |   4 +\n internal/worker/run.go           |  74 ++++++++++++++++-\n 17 files changed, 879 insertions(+), 81 deletions(-)\n\ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\nindex 9f7863d..a920c80 100644\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -366,7 +366,7 @@ A worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test r\n \n **The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\n \n-**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round's head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. This request path is off until part B of #255: the merger's `Rounds` dependency stays unset in `servecmd`, so a conflict, like a red required check, still goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n+**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round's head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. `servecmd` wires the review fix loop into the merger as `Rounds`, so a conflict starts a conflict round. In it the controller fetches `main` itself, trial-merges it into a scratch worktree (no commit, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, renormalize or `-X`), and gives the worker an untrusted `conflict.md` (capped, escaped paths; no hunks). The first agent step starts with that merge in its worktree. After the step the controller runs no git in the agent's worktree: it reads the recorded conflicted files for leftover markers (advisory), measures the PR's own diff against the fetched `main` tip, not the job's base, and hands the committer the full diff against `main` with `MergeParent` set to that tip. The code lane does not run `git merge`: it fetches `main` into a private ref and refuses unless it equals `MergeParent` (`ErrMergeParent`), requires the branch still at the job's base (`ErrConflict`, no retry), writes the declared files over main's tree, and checks that the result differs from `main` only in declared paths that pass `CheckCodePath`, so `.github/`, `.ard/`, `.gitmodules` and `.gitattributes` equal `main` byte for byte. It commits with parents `[branch tip, main tip]` and pushes with a lease on the branch tip (a merge whose tree equals main's is still committed). A GitHub rejection of a merge that brings in workflow changes ends `needs-human`. The loop counts a conflict round as landed only when the head is a merge that includes the round's head and whose second parent is on `main`. The conflict-round section of the worker prompt is a separate human PR.\n \n **The merger takes it from here.** A worker PR stays a draft until every requested review passes at its current head; then the controller marks it ready for review. The merger merges ready PRs that also pass its gates.\n \n@@ -414,7 +414,7 @@ Only ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` wit\n - every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does, unless the PR's own patch to that path is unchanged: the stable patch id of the diff from the merge base is identical before and after the update, the head reaches the reviewed commit along first parents through two-parent merges only, and no git step failed. Every merge in that chain must also have exactly